Negative control for the PAGE-SCOPE law (T3.1 falsifiability, `AC3.2` / `P1-R12`).

Makes registration GLOBAL. `register`'s `persistent` default flips from `false`
to `true`, so the `onUnmounted(() => remove(config.key))` cleanup branch is never
taken for any caller that does not pass the flag — which is every page: the
layout's `useAuth-session` section is the one call site that passes `true`
explicitly (`app/layouts/default.vue`), so it behaves identically under the
mutant and the only sections whose lifetime changes are the page-registered
ones. A section registered by `useClientEmails` therefore survives the
navigation away and is still in the registry — and so in Debug — on the next
page.

The registry IS what makes Debug page-scoped: nothing else bounds a section's
lifetime, so a default that keeps every section alive quietly turns the
page-scoped pane into a global one. Nothing looks broken at the call site — the
section still appears where it was registered, `hasSections` still answers true,
the sheet still opens on the right tab. The defect is only visible one
navigation later, which is why the mutant is a flipped default rather than a
deleted line: it is the one plausible regression on this composable, and it is
the shape the `.types.ts` contract's "removed again when the registering
component unmounts" clause exists to hold.

The mutant is deliberately NOT the removal of the `onUnmounted` call — that
leaves an unused import and reds lint before any spec runs, so the control would
be proving the linter rather than the page-scope assertion.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: the page-scope case — a section registered by a component that has since
unmounted is gone from `sections`, so a second page sees only its own. The
persistent case (the layout's session section outliving a page change) must stay
GREEN under the mutant; a red there means the patch crashed rather than widened
the lifetime, and the two are told apart by which section is the extra one.

